questions.question:
Fix this loop so it logs 0, 1, 2 without changing the callback style. Then explain the fix.

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

questions.starter_code:
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

questions.answer:
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

With var there is one function-scoped binding shared by all three callbacks. By the time the timers run, the loop has finished and i is 3, so the output is 3, 3, 3. let is block-scoped and creates a fresh binding for each iteration, so each closure captures its own i and the output is 0, 1, 2.

Before let, the fix was an IIFE: for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) { (function (j) { setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0); })(i); }. The IIFE creates a new parameter binding per call. Either way, the lesson is that closures capture bindings, not values.

