const asha = { name: "Asha" };
const rohan = { name: "Rohan" };
console.log(groupBy([asha, rohan, asha], (u) => u));

questions.answer:
const groupBy = (items, keyFn) => {
  const map = new Map();
  for (const item of items) {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    if (!map.has(key)) map.set(key, []);
    map.get(key).push(item);
  }
  return map;
};

const asha = { name: "Asha" };
const rohan = { name: "Rohan" };
console.log(groupBy([asha, rohan, asha], (u) => u));
// Map(2) { { name: 'Asha' } => [asha, asha], { name: 'Rohan' } => [rohan] }

An object version would do obj[key] ??= [] and push, but every object key is coerced to the string "[object Object]", so Asha and Rohan collapse into one bucket. Map uses SameValueZero, so object keys keep their identity and two references to asha share a bucket while rohan gets his own. Map also has size and preserves insertion order, and it does not inherit prototype keys. Convert with [...map] or Object.fromEntries(map) only when the keys are strings and you need JSON.

